[0m[0m
The three behaviors that drove the translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mbad()[0m prints [38;5;10mfffffffe[0m, not [38;5;10mfe[0m. [38;5;10mdata * 2[0m is computed in [38;5;10mint[0m (254), narrowed back to [38;5;10mchar[0m giving −2; then [38;5;10mprintf("%02x", charHex)[0m promotes that [38;5;10mchar[0m to [38;5;10mint[0m and [38;5;10m%x[0m reinterprets the bits as [38;5;10munsigned int[0m, so the value sign-extends to 32 bits and the [38;5;10m02[0m width is moot. I reproduced this as [38;5;10m(data as i32 * 2) as i8[0m followed by [38;5;10mwrite!(out, "{:02x}", promoted as u32)[0m. This is the original CWE defect, kept intact.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mgoodB2G()[0m always takes the else branch. [38;5;10mdata = ' '[0m is immediately overwritten by [38;5;10mCHAR_MAX[0m, and [38;5;10m127 < 127/2[0m (integer division, 63) is false, so the only output is the "too large" message. [38;5;10mgoodG2B()[0m prints [38;5;10m04[0m. So any nonzero input yields those two lines in that order.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mscanf("%d", &x)[0m leaves [38;5;10mx[0m at 0 on EOF or matching failure, sending those inputs to [38;5;10mbad()[0m. I hand-rolled the conversion rather than using [38;5;10mread_line[0m, since [38;5;10m%d[0m skips leading whitespace including newlines, stops at the first non-digit, and leaves the rest of the stream unconsumed. I also mirrored glibc's out-of-range handling — accumulate as [38;5;10mlong[0m, saturate at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m, then truncate to [38;5;10mint[0m. That detail is observable: [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m saturates to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m, truncating to −1 → [38;5;10mgood()[0m, while [38;5;10m4294967296[0m truncates to 0 → [38;5;10mbad()[0m. Both verified against the C binary.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One thing I could not verify: [38;5;10mchar[0m signedness and 64-bit [38;5;10mlong[0m are platform-dependent, and the constants are baked in for Linux/x86-64. On a target with unsigned [38;5;10mchar[0m (e.g. ARM by default) the C would print [38;5;10mfe[0m and this translation would not match; I assumed the reference platform.[0m[0m
